{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Separate yourselves from the midst of this congregation, etc.</b> It is plausible to hear this as follows: even at first it did not enter the mind of the Holy One, blessed be He, to punish those who had not sinned; rather, He said this concerning the congregation of Korah. But Moses understood that He had said it concerning the entire congregation of Israel, because it had already been stated, ״וַיַּקְהֵל עֲלֵיהֶם קֹרַח אֶת כָּל הָעֵדָה״, and Moses supposed that all Israel had agreed with him. Therefore he said concerning the entire congregation, ״וַאֲכַלֶּה אֹתָם כְּרָגַע״, and he was not concerned lest they say, 'Is it not within the power of HaShem, etc.' — for in any case they have already been tossed about for forty years in the wilderness, and even if He were to consume all of them, He could still make Moses and Aaron into a great nation within forty years, just as the seventy souls in Egypt suddenly became a great and mighty nation. Therefore Moses said in his prayer: ״אֵל אֱלֹהֵי הָרוּחוֹת לְכָל בָּשָׂר״ — because Moses did not perceive in Israel any friendly disposition whatsoever. For even though it is stated ״וַיַּקְהֵל עֲלֵיהֶם קֹרַח אֶת כָּל הָעֵדָה״, none the less there is no proof from here that Israel truly gathered against Moses; rather, Korah did his own thing and assembled them against them, after the manner of every enticer and seducer — and who is to say that Israel were drawn after his words? But once Moses understood that the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to consume all Israel in an instant, this is because He is Elohim of the spirits, and knows what is in the spirit of each and every one, and He knew that in their hearts they agreed with Korah. None the less Moses said, ״הָאִישׁ אֶחָד יֶחֱטָא״ — concerning Korah the seducer he said this, for he is the principal sinner in this seduction, and it is fitting that He not be wrathful against the entire congregation. For we find that it is stated (Numbers 12:1), ״וַתְּדַבֵּר מִרְיָם וְאַהֲרֹן בְּמֹשֶׁה״, and Aaron was not punished, because Miriam was the initiator of the speech and Aaron was secondary to her. So too the entire congregation — one may plead some merit on their behalf, for they did not come of their own accord to this sin, but only on account of the oppressive enticer and seducer with his smooth tongue. And if in any case they deserve some punishment, none the less justice does not dictate that they should be equal in punishment to the enticer — to consume all of them in an instant with one death. For behold, even regarding the serpent the enticer, He added to the curse beyond Adam and Eve, for all are healed except the serpent, which has no remedy (Genesis Rabbah 20:5)."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:43.253489539Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}